




    WHAT IS A UNIT TEST?
 A unit test checks that a single small piece of your code (like one function) does exactly what you expect.
Python has a built-in module called unittest — no installation needed.
____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________


    HOW TO START — 4 SIMPLE STEPS

 1. Write your functions first. For example, imagine you have a todo.py file
    where all your functions like add_task(), remove_task(), and complete_task() will reside.
    
 2. Create a separate test file. Name it test_todo.py.
    a. At the top, import unittest 
    b. import the functions file, your todo.py.
    
 3. Write a test class that inherits from unittest.TestCase.
    a. Inside it, write methods that each start with test_then_what_the_test_will_test_for(self)
       Python finds and runs them automatically.
    b. Each method calls one of your functions and then uses an assert like assertEqual() or assertTrue()
       to verify the result is what you expected.
       
 4.Run your tests from the terminal with python -m unittest test_todo.py.
    a. A dot (.) means a test passed.
    b. F means failed.
    c. E means an error/exception occurred.


___________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________


    THE CORE PATTERN INSIDE EVERY TEST (AAA)
    
 1. Arrange — set up any data you need
 2. Act — call the function you're testing
 3. Assert — check the result matches what you expected

____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________


    USEFUL ASSERT METHODS TO KNOW

 1. assertEqual(a, b)       —>          checks a == b
 2. assertTrue(x)           —>          checks x is truthy
 3. assertIn(item, list)    —>          checks item exists in the list
 4. assertRaises(Error, fn) —>          checks that a function raises a specific error


_____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

NOTE: One good habit to build: run your tests after every change. 
If something breaks, you'll know immediately which change caused it.






























